{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"8fb15e18-ac3e-422c-9c62-5ff01bf5d4b9","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The generator has been updated so that HR corridors are correctly emitted as **Instant‑HR corridors** (`{start, end}`) instead of the zone‑mapped form (`{value, end}`).  \n\n### Changes made\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| **gen.py** | In `hr_target`, the dictionary key **`value`** was renamed to **`start`**.\u003cbr\u003e```python\\nreturn {\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"start\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}\\n``` |\n| **ANSWER.md** (artifact) | Added a detailed explanation of the change and why it fixes the watch‑side clamping issue. |\n\n### Why this fixes the problem\n- The Intervals.icu API interprets `{units: \"%lthr\", value: lo, end: hi}` as a **Lap‑HR / zone‑mapped** target. Garmin then maps the corridor to the watch’s own LTHR zones, producing the incorrect clamped values you observed.\n- Using `{units: \"%lthr\", start: lo, end: hi}` signals an **Instant‑HR corridor**. The FIT export now contains the raw BPM range calculated from the athlete’s LTHR stored in Intervals, so the watch displays the intended BPM values without clamping.\n\nYou can now run the generator (e.g., `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json \u003e event.json`) and verify that the generated workout uses the correct HR target shape. The watch should show the exact BPM corridor derived from your LTHR percentages.  \n\n*All modifications were performed within the current workspace; no external services were invoked.*\n","duration_seconds":49.894702,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":27962,"output_tokens":2794,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":197824,"total_tokens":30756}}}
